WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.869
Air is a remarkable substance.

00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:04.771
Not only does it
allow us to breathe,

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:06.840
which I think we can all
agree is a good thing,

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.908
but if you compress
it and contain it,

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:12.278
you can have loads of fun.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.747
Like defying the
laws of physics.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.852
Or for wacky furniture.

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:19.986
Whack!

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:22.021
See?

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:24.457
The secret to understanding
and using compressed

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:27.594
air is to realize that
all it wants to do

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.963
is stop being compressed air.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:32.766
It wants to escape
and in so doing air

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:36.102
teaches us valuable science
on pressure differentials

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:39.172
and conservation of energy.

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:41.708
If air in one part of a
container is compressed,

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:44.010
it will apply pressure
to all other parts

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:47.313
until all areas are
the same pressure.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.350
This is due to
conservation of energy.

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:53.286
When a container of compressed
air is open to the atmosphere,

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:55.622
the high pressure
inside equalizes

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:57.824
with a lower pressure
outside, producing

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.359
a force as the air escapes.

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:04.664
And whatever is containing the
compressed air needs to have

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:06.099
the material
strength to withstand

00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:09.302
the pressure differential,
otherwise, it will fail.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.276
To sum up, pressure
changes will be transferred

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:18.211
to all parts of the container.

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:20.246
Give something at high
pressure an opening,

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:22.882
and it will take it
and escape with force.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.718
And finally, whatever you
do, make sure the container

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:27.620
you're using is strong enough.

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:29.489
So now you know.

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:30.924
But do our researchers know?

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:39.098
Yes, they do.

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:40.867
The jumper has
cleverly demonstrated

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.536
a basic pneumatic system.

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:46.573
He adds kinetic energy,
with his jump, which causes

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:49.509
a pressure change in the bag.

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:51.644
And due to
conservation of energy,

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:54.681
the kinetic energy is
transferred to her,

00:01:54.747 --> 00:02:00.119
and she then transfers that
same energy to the bedside table

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:00.920
via her head.

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:08.228
How about something a
little more high tech?

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:10.230
Surely these amateur
rocket engineers

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:11.297
understand pneumatics.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.302
Turns out, they don't.

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:18.204
The young researcher
was trying to compress

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:20.340
air by jumping on the pump.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:21.975
Unfortunately, he misses.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.781
But Oliver does it.

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.885
When the pump is pressed, the
pressure differential created

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:35.588
applies a force that
launches the rocket.

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:38.191
Good to get a closer
look at the physics.

00:02:38.258 --> 00:02:40.093
Understanding the science
allows you to take

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:41.628
advantage of the science.

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:45.298
Like building a
beach trampoline.

00:02:45.365 --> 00:02:47.967
Fun times.

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:51.404
Yeah, less fun times.

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:53.806
The membrane of the beach
ball didn't have the material

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:56.876
strength to contain the higher
pressure produced by our more

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:59.646
sure footed researcher.

